  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nScattered shwrs and isolated storms will be possible today\nacross portions of the north country as shortwave energy skirts\nthe Canadian border. Any activity that does form during the day\ntoday should be fairly isolated in nature. Meanwhile, better \nchances for more widespread rain should arrive this evening as \nmain upper trough begins sliding through the region. Models have\nbeen fairly consistent with this the past few days and NBM \npops have increased as a result. SPC Day 1 does have a marginal \nrisk for severe storm activity across western and central NY, \nwhere earlier timing (i.e. afternoon) of the incoming wave \nshould allow for more robust activity in an area of stronger \nshear aloft. Deep-layered shear drops off further east and given\nthe later arrival time of activity across eastern NY/western \nNew England, overall severe threat for our area appears limited.\nBest chances for rain based on this morning's high-res guidance\nshould be along and south of I-90 with highest precip totals \nexpected across the eastern Catskills and Mid-Hudson Valley.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA strong cold front, for this time of year anyway, is expected \nto pass across the region late Friday. On the backside of this \nboundary, high pressure will build east from western Ontario and\nthe central Great Lakes region. Clearing skies combined with \nweak northwest winds will set the stage for a cool Saturday \nmorning with low temps ranging from the mid/upper 40s across the\nhigh terrain, to middle 50s in valley locations. Overall \nconfidence remains high for this scenario as LREF grand ensemble\nmembers show widespread 80% probabilities of lows cooler than \n55F across the region. Regardless, with high pressure in firm \ncontrol along with low humidity, Saturday is shaping up to be a \nbanner day with highs climbing into the mid to upper 70s across\nthe area. Enjoy it while you can as confidence is increasing \nfor rain returning to the area on Sunday as the next cold front \nimpacts our region.",
